changes=[]
def para(s,start,new,file,why):
 i=s.index(start);end=s.find('\n\n',i);end=len(s) if end<0 else end
 old=s[i:end];changes.append(dict(file=file,reason=why,before=old,after=new));return s[:i]+new+s[end:]
p=E/'chapters/60-sns.tex';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8');n=p.name
s=para(s,'The actuator and torque models',r'''The actuator and torque models of Chapters~\ref{ch:muscles} and~\ref{ch:torque} predict how the robot's muscles produce joint motion. Studying neural control requires connecting those mechanics to a synthetic nervous system (SNS). This chapter develops a simulation framework in which neural controllers can drive the Gait2392 musculoskeletal model and separate BPA-actuated models, receive sensory feedback, and be tested under specified support and disturbance conditions. Its purpose is to make controller behavior measurable as neural organization, actuator mechanics, and mechanical support are changed. The contribution is the implemented framework and its demonstrated tests; unsupported walking remains an open control problem.''',n,'State the model, adjustable conditions, purpose, contribution, and result boundary immediately.')
s=s.replace('I adjusted applied currents, synaptic weights, and muscle parameters by hand.', 'We adjusted applied currents, synaptic weights, and muscle parameters by hand.')
s=para(s,'The AnimatLab result established',r'''The suspended motion left an important question unresolved: could the controller maintain coordinated activity when the model contacted the ground and had to support load? Answering it required control over the amount and direction of mechanical support, access to sensory and motor signals, and repeatable changes to the controller or body. Those requirements motivated the framework developed here. The Li-derived W2L walker, the Gait2392 human model, and the BPA-routed robot model are distinct mechanical models; a result obtained with one is identified accordingly.''',n,'Replace abstract missing-link language with the load-bearing question and the controls needed to study it.')
start=s.index('The suspended walker raised');end=s.index(r'\section{Software Tools}',start)
old=s[start:end]
new=r'''The project lacked a tested way to connect its neural controllers to the human reference and artificial-muscle models and evaluate them under comparable conditions. The missing capability was more specific than generating rhythmic joint motion. I needed to choose a controller, define the mechanical model and sensory inputs, specify how the body was supported, and measure what changed after a controlled intervention. Without those controls, an improvement in motion could not be assigned to neural feedback, muscle mechanics, or external support.

I addressed this gap by connecting OpenSim-derived models to MuJoCo and SNS-Toolbox, implementing a BPA-force calculation, and adding procedures for support, stimulation, perturbation, and parameter search. On the human reference model, alternative neural organizations can be evaluated against the same mechanical description. Separate W2L and BPA-actuated models extend the workflow to different bodies and actuators. The SNS-Simscape library provides graphical circuit construction and component demonstrations in MATLAB. These environments serve different parts of the work; importing a mechanism into Simscape is a separate achievement from controlling it with the demonstrated circuits.

The framework is evaluated at three levels. Circuit tests ask whether a controller produces the intended neural response. Interface tests ask whether activation, muscle force, and sensory measurements pass correctly between the neural and mechanical models. Coupled tests ask how the resulting motion changes with support, sensory pathways, or disturbances. The results below demonstrate each level within a stated scope. They establish a working basis for controlled neuromechanical experiments while identifying the balance, model-transfer, and controller-tuning problems that remain.

Existing tools supply the components of this framework. OpenSim provides musculoskeletal geometry, MyoConverter translates that geometry to MuJoCo, and SNS-Toolbox supplies neuron and synapse models \citep{delp_opensim_2007, caggiano_myosuite_2022, todorov_mujoco_2012, nourse_sns_2023}. MuJoCo supports scripted dynamics, contact, and repeated evaluations; the version used here models rigid tendons. Simulink supplies graphical circuit assembly, and Simscape Multibody supplies mechanical imports, including CAD-derived mechanisms. AnimatLab contains the original walker and reference circuits. My contribution connects these components through the model conversions, actuator calculations, circuit implementations, and tests described below.

'''
changes.append(dict(file=n,reason='Replace abstract gap taxonomy with the concrete experimental capability and how each environment contributes.',before=old,after=new));s=s[:start]+new+s[end:]
mark=r'\subsection{Graphical Circuit Construction}'
i=s.index(mark)
new=r'''\subsection{Support, Stimulation, and Parameter Search}\label{sec:sns_experiment_controls}

The MuJoCo simulations allow mechanical support to be specified independently of the neural controller. A virtual harness applies forces and torques to the body: it can hold the model above the ground, lower it into contact, retain vertical support, or constrain selected directions of translation and rotation. These settings represent different experimental conditions. Suspended motion tests whether the controller and actuators can move the limbs, whereas supported ground contact adds loading and sensory feedback. Progression with retained support is reported separately from unsupported walking.

The training sequence, or curriculum, progresses from simpler conditions toward greater mechanical demand. Its stages include air stepping, supported standing, ground-contact stepping, and reduced support. Parameters can be held fixed to test transfer between stages or re-tuned at a new stage to seek a viable configuration. These procedures answer different questions: successful re-tuning shows that a configuration can be found, while a fixed-controller test shows whether an existing controller tolerates the change. A stage is demonstrated only when its stated motion and stability criteria are met.

Neural interventions include tonic currents, brief stimuli to selected circuit populations, and removal of specified feedback or coupling pathways. Mechanical interventions include timed forces at the pelvis and changes to segment mass and inertia. The recorded outputs include membrane voltage, actuator force, joint motion, foot contact, body position and orientation, and harness force. Together, these controls allow the neural response and the resulting motion to be examined in the same experiment.

Optuna automates parameter searches within specified bounds. Each candidate is evaluated using measures such as bilateral contact cycles, inter-leg phase, forward displacement, joint trajectories, posture, and survival. The score determines which candidate is selected; a separate replay checks that candidate under the stated conditions. Optuna is used as a search tool, without a claim that it finds a global optimum or outperforms other optimizers. Interpretation depends on the objective and validity checks, including confirmation that every searched parameter reaches the implemented model.

'''
changes.append(dict(file=n,reason='Explain harnesses, curriculum, neural and external interventions, outputs, and Optuna role in reader-facing terms.',before='',after=new));s=s[:i]+new+s[i:]
s=para(s,'The coupled W2L tests ask',r'''The W2L tests examine whether a controller retains bilateral contact cycling when the body or selected neural pathways change. An implementation check showed that the initial search applied only six of the 61 saved parameter fields; the other 55 fields did not reach the model. Those records describe a controller with the remaining pathways at their default strengths. The results reported here use a later replay with the stored individual fields applied. Because those values had not been optimized under the corrected mapping, this replay tests a candidate controller rather than a newly optimized solution.''',n,'Keep the consequential parameter limitation once, in plain language, without making the results read like a debugging chronology.')
s=para(s,'After correcting the runner',r'''With the stored individual fields applied and settings held fixed, the control completed 17 left and 16 right heel-contact cycles during the \qty{16}{\second} record (Figure~\ref{fig:w2l_perturbation}). Removing the crossed heel pathway produced 16/16 cycles, the Ia chain 13/13, the Renshaw pathways 16/14, the heel cascade 17/15, and commissural coupling 13/12. Removing Ib load feedback produced no heel-contact cycles on either side. The specific result is that Ib feedback was necessary for contact cycling in this configuration, while commissural removal reduced the count without abolishing it. Contact counts alone do not show that phase, posture, or gait quality remained unchanged.''',n,'Lead the deletion result with its observation and model-specific interpretation.')
s=para(s,'The initial AnimatLab result showed',r'''The framework addresses the project's model-integration gap by making neural control, muscle mechanics, sensory return, and support conditions explicit and adjustable. It connects the Gait2392 reference model to neural controllers, provides force calculations for BPA-actuated models, and supplies procedures for staged tuning and controlled interventions. Graphical circuit construction in Simulink complements the scripted evaluations. These capabilities allow a controller to be examined at the circuit, interface, and coupled-model levels before a whole-model result is interpreted.''',n,'End with what was built and why the demonstrated capabilities matter.')
s=para(s,'The new results strengthen',r'''The tests show what that framework can establish. The Rybak-derived neural model reproduced selected phase-resetting behaviors, the contact-driven rhythm core agreed across implementations, and the BPA gate demonstrated air stepping and supported standing with the actuator force model inside the simulation. W2L pathway tests linked Ib feedback to bilateral contact cycling at one operating point. They also showed why cycling and walking must be distinguished: the corrected mass-change candidate exceeded the posture criterion in nine of ten conditions. The Li transcription changes the original neuron dynamics, and the Simulink air-stepping mechanics do not yet follow the RG period. These limits identify specific model and control problems for subsequent work.''',n,'Connect representative positive results and specific limitations to the experimental capability.')
s=para(s,'The resulting contribution is',r'''The present contribution is a usable framework for developing and testing neuromechanical controllers, with demonstrated components and explicit limits. Establishing a general biological mechanism will require matched interventions and comparisons with the corresponding biological or published-model results. Achieving unsupported walking will require further balance and weight-bearing control. The framework makes those questions accessible to controlled simulation; it does not resolve them by the existence of a tuned trajectory. Chapter~\ref{ch:futurework} discusses the remaining integration and hardware work.''',n,'State the engineering contribution directly and reserve biological generalization for its required evidence.')
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
