p=E/'chapters/15-background.tex';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8');n=p.name
s=para(s,"The two-level organization is the field's",r'''Two-level models separate the timing of the locomotor rhythm from the distribution of activity to muscles. This distinction has been extended to genetically identified interneuron classes \citep{rybak_organization_2015} and circuits controlling muscles that cross two joints \citep{shevtsova_modeling_2016}. The latter problem also arises in a robot: activating a two-joint muscle can affect motion at both joints, so its control cannot be assigned to either joint in isolation.

Coupling the circuit to a musculoskeletal model adds the effects of mechanics and sensory feedback. Such models show how afferent feedback shapes CPG output \citep{markin_afferent_2010} and how body mechanics and feedback influence the behavior of different CPG architectures \citep{deng_biomechanical_2022}. Human locomotion models combine locomotor primitives with Ia, II, and Ib pathways and Renshaw cells to examine their contributions to control \citep{dirusso_investigating_2023}. Other models examine interlimb responses to sudden loss of ground support \citep{shinohara_mechanisms_2025}. Section~\ref{sec:bg_compneuro} describes these circuits, and Chapter~\ref{ch:sns} develops tools for implementing and testing related controllers.''',n,'Replace compressed circuit taxonomy and vague convergence language with concrete neural/mechanical relationships.')
s=para(s,'The third line, in which this dissertation sits,',r'''The third line uses biologically grounded synthetic nervous systems with explicit neuron and synapse models \citep{nourse_sns_2023}. Their circuit structure allows a researcher to change a specified pathway and examine its effect on behavior. Related work includes motor-module bipedal walkers \citep{ichimura_pathological_2019}, two-layer RG/PF control of a musculoskeletal macaque model \citep{nishizaki_gait_2026}, and two-level CPG control of muscle-driven robotic hind legs \citep{fukuoka_autonomous_2021}. This dissertation develops the interfaces needed to study such controllers with the project's human reference and artificial-muscle models. The actuator and knee-torque experiments provide mechanical evidence, while the simulation framework provides a way to examine controller behavior under specified support and perturbation conditions.''',n,'Replace unsupported field-wide exclusivity claim with the scoped contribution of this project.')
s=para(s,'Figure~\\ref{fig:decerebrate_cat} shows what that accounting',r'''Figure~\ref{fig:decerebrate_cat} documents the animal preparation behind many foundational spinal-locomotion experiments \citep{brown_intrinsic_1911, decerebrate_cat_footage}. In this preparation, the forebrain is disconnected by a brainstem transection while spinal and brainstem circuits continue to produce motor activity. The image is included to make the experimental context visible alongside the circuit schematics. Robotic and computational models can support preliminary intervention tests, but their relevance to these biological preparations depends on reproducing the observations being studied.''',n,'Replace the unclear accounting metaphor with the literal purpose of the figure; preserve its historical and ethical context.')
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
